# Kalainova — Development Guardrails

This project follows strict quality, architectural, and security boundaries defined in `AGENTS.md`.

## Public Repository Rules
- This repository is public. Never commit real credentials, character identities, account handles, brand names, or real media assets (`mp3`, `mp4`, `wav`, `m4a`, `mov`, `webm`, reference images).
- Use `$KALAINOVA_HOME` for private studio jobs and kits.

## Verification & Quality Gates
- Always run `just ci` to verify changes.
- Never skip git hooks or bypass pre-commit verification checks.
- Never lower the coverage floor (`fail_under` or `--cov-fail-under` in `pyproject.toml`).
- Keep all Python source and test files strictly under 500 lines (`max_file_lines = 500`).
- Maintain gate integrity in `justfile`: never diverge `.github/workflows/ci.yaml` from `just ci` sequence.

## Architecture & Code Boundaries
- Preserve import boundaries enforced by `.importlinter`.
- When adding or modifying top-level modules under `src/kalainova/`, update `ARCHITECTURE.md` with a matching section and update the Component Diagram in the same change (`just ci-arch`).
- No inline prompt literals >200 characters (place in `prompts/`).
- No bare `# noqa` or `# type: ignore` without a specific code and justification.

## Human Gates
- Humans gate publish (`kalainova approve`). Agents never write `approval.json`.
- Humans sign off spend (`kalainova sign-off`). Agents build packages (`kalainova package`) but never sign them off.

## Commits
- Use conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `chore:`, `refactor:`, `test:`.
- No `Co-authored-by` / `Co-Authored-By` trailers in commits.
